          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-89",
              "name": "SQL injection in app.py search() via unparameterized LIKE clause",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL injection in app.py search() via unparameterized LIKE clause"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `search` function in `app.py` builds its SQL query by concatenating the user-controlled `query` argument directly into a `LIKE` clause, allowing SQL injection. The parameterized alternative `search_v2` is not vulnerable, but `search` is independently reachable and injectable."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `search` function in `app.py` builds its SQL query by concatenating the user-controlled `query` argument directly into a `LIKE` clause, allowing SQL injection. The parameterized alternative `search_v2` is not vulnerable, but `search` is independently reachable and injectable.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker (the query originates from an HTTP query string) can read the entire database, including products where `published=0`, defeating the documented access-control filter. Demonstrated locally: the injected payload returned the hidden row `(2, 'unreleased book')` and dumped the full schema via a UNION against `sqlite_master`.\n\nReplace the string-concatenated query in `search` with the parameterized form already implemented in `search_v2`: pass the user-supplied value as a bound parameter (`db.execute('SELECT id,name FROM products WHERE published=1 AND name LIKE ?', ('%' + query + '%',))`) instead of interpolating it into the SQL string. If `search_v2` is intended as its replacement, remove or gate the legacy `search` function so no reachable code path builds SQL by concatenation; the demonstrated impact is bypass of the `published` access-control filter plus arbitrary read of the database (schema and data disclosure).",
                "markdown": "The `search` function in `app.py` builds its SQL query by concatenating the user-controlled `query` argument directly into a `LIKE` clause, allowing SQL injection. The parameterized alternative `search_v2` is not vulnerable, but `search` is independently reachable and injectable.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker (the query originates from an HTTP query string) can read the entire database, including products where `published=0`, defeating the documented access-control filter. Demonstrated locally: the injected payload returned the hidden row `(2, 'unreleased book')` and dumped the full schema via a UNION against `sqlite_master`.\n\nReplace the string-concatenated query in `search` with the parameterized form already implemented in `search_v2`: pass the user-supplied value as a bound parameter (`db.execute('SELECT id,name FROM products WHERE published=1 AND name LIKE ?', ('%' + query + '%',))`) instead of interpolating it into the SQL string. If `search_v2` is intended as its replacement, remove or gate the legacy `search` function so no reachable code path builds SQL by concatenation; the demonstrated impact is bypass of the `published` access-control filter plus arbitrary read of the database (schema and data disclosure)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
            },
