          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "12ee397e5a822bd0a5eb7eee600cd9c8ef896f79d043adcb419ba5f7ec65bbd2"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "091274e2ad40f12c26f944cb00be2712c536b9e81137676e98311bc2ccf04d16",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-20 07:27:59 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/catalog (local project)",
              "cwe": "CWE-89",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker (the query originates from an HTTP query string) can read the entire database, including products where `published=0`, defeating the documented access-control filter. Demonstrated locally: the injected payload returned the hidden row `(2, 'unreleased book')` and dumped the full schema via a UNION against `sqlite_master`.",
              "technical_analysis": "`search` executes `\"SELECT id,name FROM products WHERE published=1 AND name LIKE '%\" + query + \"%'\"`, where `query` originates from an HTTP query string and is concatenated into the SQL text without any validation or escaping. A payload containing a single quote terminates the string literal, allowing arbitrary SQL to be injected. The `published=1` predicate — the application's documented access control for hiding unpublished products — is bypassable because the injected expression (`OR 1=1`) short-circuits it, and a UNION clause allows reading any other table in the database. `search_v2` demonstrates the correct pattern (placeholder binding), confirming the vulnerability is specific to the concatenating implementation and that both functions are individually reviewable versions of the same feature.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the string-concatenated query in `search` with the parameterized form already implemented in `search_v2`: pass the user-supplied value as a bound parameter (`db.execute('SELECT id,name FROM products WHERE published=1 AND name LIKE ?', ('%' + query + '%',))`) instead of interpolating it into the SQL string. If `search_v2` is intended as its replacement, remove or gate the legacy `search` function so no reachable code path builds SQL by concatenation; the demonstrated impact is bypass of the `published` access-control filter plus arbitrary read of the database (schema and data disclosure).",
              "counterevidence": "Actively looked for mitigations: no input validation, sanitization, escaping, or allowlisting is applied to `query` anywhere between the caller and `db.execute`; the query is used verbatim in string concatenation. The only control in the file is the parameterization in `search_v2`, which is a separate function and does not run on the `search` path. Both normal and attack inputs were executed locally to confirm the differential.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that `search` is exposed on a deployed, internet-reachable endpoint (rather than only invoked internally as in this local synthetic test) would raise exploitability confidence from demonstrated-local to full remote; nothing would lower it short of removing the concatenation.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Import `app` in a local Python process and create the in-memory database with `app.database()`.\\n2. Call `app.search(db, \"book\")` to establish the baseline: only the published product `(1, 'public book')` is returned.\\n3. Call `app.search(db, \"book' OR 1=1 --\")` and observe the unpublished product `(2, 'unreleased book')` is also returned, proving the `published=1` filter is bypassed via injection.\\n4. Call `app.search(db, \"x' UNION SELECT 1, sql FROM sqlite_master --\")` and observe the injected UNION extracts the full schema from `sqlite_master`.\\n5. Run the same payloads against `app.search_v2(db, ...)` and observe they return no rows, confirming the parameterized variant is not injectable and isolating the defect to `search`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
